# ABOUT THIS FORK

This forlk mainly introduces public APIs for Green Threads and
MultiCore support, but also includes bug fixes which may not have
landed (yet?) upstream.

## Threads in Perl

Following the ordinary computer-science definition (as summarised on Wikipedia):
a **thread of execution** is the smallest sequence of programmed instructions
that can be managed independently by a scheduler. Multiple threads within a
single process run *concurrently* and *fully share the process's memory* and
resources (address space, file descriptors, globals), in contrast to separate
processes, which do not share those resources. A thread is characterised by
three things:

1. an independent **flow of control** — its own instruction pointer and call
   stack;
2. **shared memory** with the other threads of the process; and
3. **lightweight lifecycle management** — cheap to create, and cheap for a
   scheduler to start, suspend, resume, switch between, and join.

MultiCore parallelism is the ability to run **simultaneous execution on multiple
CPU cores**.
It is not part of the definition of a thread. A thread requires *concurrency*
which is a *structuring* property. *Parallelism* is a *performance* property,
and an additional capability layered on top.
Parallelism is routinely implemented in Perl using processes.

Perl supports two flavors of Pure-Perl threads, both green threads:
* Coro, the stackful green threads,
* Future::AwaitAsync, the stackless green threads.

And two flavors of XS threads:
* Coro::Multicore, allowing the Perl interpreter to run concurrently with XS code,
* C-level threads within XS: OS threads internally implemented in XS (eg PDL),
  but blocking the Perl interpreter while executing.

A number of CPAN modules already support Multicore. More, or improved, versions
are available in this fork repository as well. See also Coro::Multicore documentation
for other sources of Multicore-enabled modules.

Note that Perl "ithreads" are not threads per the definition given above.
`perldoc threads` officially discourages its use for new code. It is, in effect,
"processes that look like threads," with most of the cost of processes and little
of the convenience of threads.

## Why Thread APIs

This fork provides public APIs to support Green Threads and MultiCore.
Those APIs are used by the forked versions of Coro, Future::AwaitAsync and
Coro::Multicore.

The upstream versions of those modules already provide threads on top of the
upstream Perl. What this fork brings is **stabitily**, as the upstream thread
modules both suffer from relying on Perl internals which Perl5 Porters don't
want (legitimately) to support as public APIs.
In the past, Coro has been broken many times by Perl internal changes, which
motivated this fork. The last dating from Perl 5.24, and still not fixed in
Coro 6.57.

See Porting/green_threads_and_execstate.md for all the details.

